--- log ---
2026-05-03T00:00Z claude created — design decision: erg works offline in isolated VMs; remote forge refs are always unknown; drop gh-specific syntax

2026-05-04T18:06Z claude closed — implemented — forge-agnostic grammar replaces gh: / gh#N; tests pass; forge refs now blocking in ready
--- body ---
## Decision

erg's contract is offline-first. Remote forge refs never resolve —
`erg ready` treats any forge ref as unknown (blocking). There is no
resolver, no network calls, no auth chain.

The `gh:` and `gh#` schemes from 0017 are GitHub-specific. Replace
them with a forge-agnostic form. `gh#N` (same-repo shorthand) is
dropped entirely — same-repo issues are not a supported ref form.

## New grammar

```
ref        := local-ref | forge-ref
local-ref  := [0-9]{4}
forge-ref  := host "/" owner "/" repo "#" number
host       := [A-Za-z0-9]([A-Za-z0-9.-]*[A-Za-z0-9])?
owner      := [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+
repo       := [A-Za-z0-9_.-]+
number     := [1-9][0-9]*
```

Examples:
- `github.com/anthropics/claude-code#1234`
- `gitlab.com/someorg/somerepo#42`
- `codeberg.org/user/project#7`

No scheme prefix. The hostname is the forge identity.
Owner and repo use a loose pattern — forge-specific validation
is not erg's job.

## `erg ready` policy

Any `forge-ref` blocker → `unknown` → blocking (fail-closed).
No flag to override in v1. The ticket is blocked until its
forge-ref blockers are removed or replaced with local refs.

Output when a forge ref is blocking:

```
0021 ship feature X    (blocked: github.com/anthropics/claude-code#1234 [unknown])
```

## Scope

In:
- `rules/tickets.md` grammar update (remove `gh:` and `gh#` forms,
  add `forge-ref` form).
- Go parser/validator: recognize `forge-ref`, reject old forms.
- `erg ready`: forge refs → unknown → blocked.
- Migrate existing `.erg` files using `gh:owner/repo#N` →
  `github.com/owner/repo#N`.

Out:
- Any network calls. Forge refs are syntax only.
- `--resolve`, `--offline`, `--assume-unknown` flags.
- Graph rendering of forge refs. (0019 — update separately.)

## JSON output (`erg ready --json`)

Each blocked ticket includes a structured `blocked_by` array so
LLM agents can act on forge refs without parsing prose:

```json
{
  "id": "0021",
  "title": "ship feature X",
  "ready": false,
  "blocked_by": [
    {"kind": "local",  "id": "0017"},
    {"kind": "forge",  "ref": "github.com/anthropics/claude-code#1234"}
  ]
}
```

`kind` is `"local"` or `"forge"`. Forge entries carry the full
ref string; the caller resolves it however it sees fit.

## Tests

- `github.com/foo/bar#1` parses as forge-ref.
- `gitlab.com/org/repo#99` parses as forge-ref.
- `gh:foo/bar#1` rejected (old form).
- `gh#42` rejected (old form).
- Bare `#42` rejected.
- `forge-ref` with zero number rejected.
- `erg ready`: ticket with forge-ref blocker is not-ready.
- `erg ready --json`: forge blocker appears as `{"kind":"forge","ref":"..."}`.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `rules/tickets.md` documents new grammar; old `gh:` / `gh#`
      forms removed.
- [ ] Go parser recognizes `forge-ref`; old forms rejected with
      precise error.
- [ ] `erg ready` plain-text marks forge-ref blockers as unknown (blocking).
- [ ] `erg ready --json` emits `blocked_by` array with `kind`/`ref` fields.
- [ ] No `gh:` or `gh#` refs remain in any `Blocked-by:` header
      across `tickets/`.
- [ ] Tests cover each form, each rejected form, and JSON output.
